CPU and Memory
The CPU (Central Processing Unit) is the main processing component of a computer that executes instructions by performing arithmetic, logic, and control operations, while memory stores data and program instructions temporarily or permanently for use during execution.

Types of Memory
Cache Memory

It is a high-speed memory located on or very close to the CPU
Provides extremely fast access to frequently used data and instructions, improving CPU performance.
Main Memory (RAM - Random Access Memory)

Temporary storage used to hold programs and data currently being processed.
Data stored in RAM is lost when the computer is turned off.
When a program is executed, it is loaded from secondary storage into RAM for faster access by the CPU.
Secondary Storage (Hard Disk)

Permanent storage for data and programs.
Data remains stored even when the computer is powered off.
Examples include internal and external hard disks, SSDs, CDs, DVDs, and USB drives.
Role of the Operating System
The Operating System (OS) is essential software that manages a computer’s hardware and software resources.

Controls how the CPU, memory, and input/output devices work together.
Manages system resources so programs run smoothly.
Acts as a link between hardware and software.
Ensures efficient and stable operation of the computer.
